course: everyday
lesson: 474
title: Feuds between families
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A feud is a long quarrel between families or groups, sometimes lasting years after everyone has forgotten how it started. Kieran's family and Jake's family haven't spoken since a row about a parking space three years ago, and the boys are told not to play together. Then they land in the same class and get on, quietly, keeping it secret at home. It feels horrible. Jake's mum says «Don't hang around with Kieran» — only because of his family — until she meets him at a party, sees how polite he is, and softens a bit. One day Kieran asks his mum why she won't talk to Jake's mum. She opens her mouth, shuts it: «D'you know, love, I can't really remember.» Next morning she says «Morning» at the school gate, and Jake's mum says «Morning» back. It's a start.
explain: Kids often pay for grown-ups' rows. Two families on Sam's street go to war over a hedge, with solicitor's letters (a solicitor is a kind of lawyer), and the two best mates are banned from playing. They still play football at school and wave from their bedroom windows. Lily's and Chloe's mums fall out over a message in the class WhatsApp; the girls write a note, «Please make up, we miss each other», and the mums read it, laugh and meet for coffee.
explain: Some feuds end with one kind move. Maddie's nan and Mrs Dobbs haven't spoken for twenty years («She knows what she did», nobody else does). When Nan gets pneumonia, a bad chest illness, Mrs Dobbs leaves soup on the step. Nan cries, and they talk over the fence again. On Chloe's road two Irish grannies have feuded since a 1997 wedding: «That eejit never paid for the cake!» (an eejit is an idiot). «Sure the cake was stale!» At a christening they end up singing together, crying, and it's over.
explain: Some are only a game: Mia's uncle in Texas yells over the fence every Fourth of July, «Y'all's brisket is drier than the Alamo!», and the cook-off ends with everyone eating both. Connor has never met his cousins, because his dad and his uncle haven't spoken for eight years; he finally meets them at his grandma's funeral, and they get on straight away. And some never end. Sam's grandad and his brother Roy fell out for forty years over their mum's clock. When Roy dies, Grandad doesn't go to the funeral. Later he tells Sam, «I'd give that clock away ten times to have one more pint with him.»
fact: A feud is a long quarrel between families or groups; it can last years after the reason is forgotten.
fact: Children are often caught in the middle of grown-ups' feuds, even though they did nothing wrong.
fact: It is fine to tell a parent honestly how a family feud makes you feel.
fact: Children can stay friends even when their parents have fallen out.
fact: One kind act, like bringing soup to someone ill, can end a feud.
fact: Asking «why are we still fighting?» can help grown-ups see a feud has no reason left.
fact: It is fairer to judge a person by what they do, not by their family.
fact: Sad events like funerals sometimes bring families back together, but it is better not to wait for one.
fact: Some feuds are never mended, and people often regret it for the rest of their lives.
fact: A joke feud, like a cooking contest between neighbours, is fine as long as everyone is in on the joke.
fact: «Y'all» is Southern US for «you all»; «eejit» is Irish for idiot; «sure» at the start of an Irish sentence just means «well».
exercise: What is a feud? | A long quarrel between families or groups | A quick argument over a game that ends by teatime | A kind of party between neighbours | A type of football match
exercise: What started the feud between Kieran's and Jake's families? | A row about a parking space | A broken window | A stolen bike from the shed | A football match on telly
exercise: How did Kieran and Jake feel about keeping their friendship secret? | It felt horrible | It felt exciting and fun | They didn't care at all | They loved having secrets
exercise: Why did Maddie's nan and Mrs Dobbs start talking again? | Mrs Dobbs brought soup when Nan was ill | Nan won a big shouting match over the fence | They both moved house | A judge told them to
exercise: Two families on Sam's street fought over a hedge. What did the two boys do? | Kept playing at school and waved from their windows | Joined in with their families and hated each other for years | Cut the hedge down at night | Never saw each other again
exercise: How did Lily and Chloe help their mums make up? | They wrote them a note | They cancelled every single sleepover till it was sorted | They argued in the WhatsApp group | They told the teacher to sort it out
exercise: Why hadn't Connor met his cousins? | His dad and his uncle had not spoken for eight years | His cousins lived on the Moon | His cousins were too young to travel anywhere | He had never wanted to meet them, not once
exercise: Where did Connor finally meet his cousins? | At his grandma's funeral | At a football match | At school | On holiday in Spain
exercise: Mia's uncle and the neighbours have a «brisket feud». What kind of feud is it? | A fun one where everyone's in on the joke | A dangerous one with fights in the street | A secret one nobody knows about | A legal one with solicitors and court letters
exercise: Why did Jake's mum say «don't hang around with Kieran»? | She judged him by his family | Kieran had been unkind to Jake | Kieran was much older | Kieran lived too far away
exercise: What changed Jake's mum's mind, a bit? | Meeting Kieran and seeing he was polite | Kieran's mum paid her | Jake cried in his room for a week | The teacher sent a letter home to her about it
exercise: How did the two Irish grannies' feud end? | They sang together at a christening | A judge ruled about the cake | One granny moved to Spain | They had a cake-throwing fight
exercise: Sam's grandad and Roy fought over what? | Their mum's clock | A football match on telly | A parking space outside | A cake at a wedding
exercise: What did Grandad say after Roy died? | He'd give the clock away to have one more pint with him | He was glad he'd kept the clock, because it was worth a lot | He didn't care at all | Roy should have said sorry first
exercise: When Kieran asked his mum why she wouldn't talk to Jake's mum, what did she find? | She couldn't remember why | It was about money they owed | It was Jake's fault, from years ago | It was a secret she couldn't tell
test: «your lot and their lot don't talk, so you can't be mates with him, end of». Is that fair on the kids? | No, kids don't have to carry grown-ups' rows | Yes, kids must join every family feud | Yes, families always know best about friends | No, so the kids should fight too
test: «I'm fixin' to tell Mom the whole hedge war is dumb». («fixin' to» is Southern US for «about to».) Is it OK to tell a parent how a feud feels? | Yes, honestly and calmly | No, kids must never mention it | No, only write it on the wall | Yes, by shouting at the neighbours
test: What would probably happen if nobody in a feud ever made a kind move? | It could go on for years | It would end by itself next week | Everyone would just forget and be friends | The kids would sort it out by themselves at once
test: «his family are trouble, so he must be trouble too». What's a better way to think? | Judge him by what he does himself | Families are all exactly the same | Never trust anyone with a big family | Ask his worst enemy about him
test: «Sure the cake was stale anyway!» What is this feud really about? | An old wedding argument, now half a joke | A crime that went to court and a judge decided | A secret about money | A football rivalry
test: Sam: «Grandad, why didn't you just make up with Roy?» What's the lesson from Grandad's story? | Don't wait, some feuds can never be mended later | Clocks and things matter more than brothers do, in the end | Feuds always end happily | Brothers never really fall out
test: In a cartoon, two families fight forever and it's hilarious. In real life, what often happens to the kids? | They get caught in the middle and feel sad | They find it hilarious every time the grown-ups row | They get extra presents | Nothing, kids don't notice
test: Kieran's mum and Jake's mum say «Morning» to each other. Why does this small thing matter? | A tiny step can start ending a feud | It means they're best friends now | It doesn't matter at all | It means the feud is getting worse
test: «Y'all's brisket is drier than the Alamo!» Is Mia's family in a real war? | No, it's a joke feud everyone enjoys | Yes, they're about to call the police | Yes, they never speak to the neighbours | No, they don't know the neighbours
test: A friend says, «My nan's not spoken to her neighbour for twenty years. It'll never change.» Is that always true? | No, one kind move can end even an old feud | Yes, old feuds can never end once they're that long | Yes, after ten years it's too late | No, but only if a judge decides
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a feud between families? | A protracted dispute which may persist after its origin is forgotten | A brief disagreement concluded within a day | A sporting fixture between two households | A dispute confined exclusively to children
exam: Identify the principal means, as established in the lesson, by which the twenty-year dispute between two elderly neighbours was concluded. | An act of kindness during the illness of one party | A judgment delivered by a court | The relocation of one party abroad | The intervention of the municipal council
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning children whose parents are engaged in a feud. | Such children may remain friends notwithstanding the dispute | Such children are obliged to perpetuate the dispute of their parents | Such children invariably share their parents' hostility | Such children bear responsibility for the dispute
exam: Ascertain the conclusion most appropriately drawn from the account of two brothers estranged for forty years over a clock. | Delay in reconciliation may result in lasting regret | Possessions are of greater value than relations | Estrangement invariably resolves itself | The clock was correctly retained
